AI Clinical Trial Card: The Impact of AI on Developer Productivity: Evidence from GitHub Copilot

Authors: Shuai Peng et al. (MIT / Microsoft Research)

AI Model Details

Provider: GitHub / OpenAI
Model Name and Version: GitHub Copilot (based on OpenAI Codex; exact model version not specified)

Access Method:

GitHub Copilot integrated directly into the developer’s code editor (Visual Studio Code)

Suggestions surfaced inline as developers typed code

Fine-tuning:

Yes. Copilot is built on Codex, a model fine-tuned on large-scale public code repositories

No task-specific fine-tuning conducted for this experiment

Prompting / Configuration Parameters:

Default Copilot configuration

No explicit control over temperature or decoding parameters reported

Performance on Standard Benchmarks:

Not reported in the paper

The study treats Copilot as a deployed commercial system rather than benchmarking the underlying model

Human Participant Profile

Sample Size:

95 participants

Population / Expertise:

Software developers recruited online

Participants had prior programming experience and were capable of completing a non-trivial Python programming task

Domain Expertise:

Python programming

Web server implementation

AI / LLM Familiarity:

Mixed and not deeply characterized

Study conducted when GitHub Copilot was relatively new, so many participants were likely first-time or early users

Training / Onboarding:

Participants received brief instructions on how to use Copilot

No extensive training period or prolonged exposure

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Participants were asked to implement a Python HTTP server that satisfied a set of functional requirements

Task required writing working, executable code rather than answering conceptual questions

Interface:

Visual Studio Code IDE

Copilot suggestions appeared inline and could be accepted, modified, or ignored

AI Role / Degree of Autonomy:

Assistive

Copilot generated code suggestions, but humans retained full control over what code was accepted

Experimental Conditions:

Control: Copilot disabled

Treatment: Copilot enabled

Primary Outcome Measures:

Task completion time

Task success rate (whether the submitted code passed correctness checks)

Main Effects:

Developers using Copilot completed the task approximately 55% faster than those without Copilot

Success rates were comparable across conditions, suggesting speed gains did not come at the cost of correctness

Notes on Interpretation

Results are strongly shaped by:

A well-scoped, relatively short programming task

Inline, low-friction AI assistance embedded directly in the coding environment

Findings may not generalize to:

Long-term software projects

Tasks requiring deep architectural reasoning

Developers with extensive prior Copilot experience